"2845","dhá","two (as adjective)
<small>dhá chathaoir = two chairs</small>
<small>dhá uair = two times</small>
<small>dhá cheann = two things</small>
<small>in dhá áit = in two places</small>
<li>an dá = the two
<small>freagair an dá cheist = answer the two questions</small>
<small>an dá úll dearg = the two red apples</small>
<aside>use <b>a dó</b> when counting, e.g. 'a haon, a dó' = one, two and when referring to the number two:
<small>a dó a chlog = two o' clock</small>
<small>uimhir a dó = number two</small>
</aside>
<aside>Irish has something called a 'dual' which is between singular and plural, which is why you say <b>an dá</b> instead of <strikethrough>na dá</strikethrough></aside>
<aside>use <b>beirt</b> for counting people</aside>","dá",6802,"Numerical Adjective","Conjunction","nf
a","an dá<br>
dhá cheann",0,0,0,NA,"","",0,FALSE,NA
